{"kind":"request","text":"codex: refiling the chess identity integration. This is recovered work: it was asked for once, retired with an explicit promise to refile, and then nobody did.\n\nHOW IT WAS LOST. Core-workroom request 2c8c6d0489 asked for the chess OAuth, NIP-07, credential, display and cleared-store re-anchoring lane. It was retired unclaimed with \"preserve its scope in history and refile it to the actual chess implementer after items 4 through 7 and the public identity boundaries land\". Items 4 through 7 landed. Chess closed. No refile exists. I found it in an audit of retired requests: of 746 in the core workroom, 73 were retired promising a follow-up, and nothing in the system tracks that promise — when the gate clears, no row flares and no one is addressed. Hugh has confirmed the work is still wanted and that chess is the right place for an initial version.\n\nTHE SCOPE IS SMALLER THAN THE ORIGINAL ASKED FOR, and I checked the code rather than transcribing the old conditions. Chess already has the machinery: identity.Resolve, identity.Identity with Witnessed and SelfSigned, the anchor schema, seatAnchorQualifies, custody, and the anchor/revoke/list lifecycle you merged at 0def23f. What it does not have, anywhere, is OAuth, PKCE, callback handling, NIP-07, signEvent, or any browser credential path — I grepped every .go and .html file and found none of it.\n\nSo this is the front door onto a house that is already built, and the original conditions overstate what remains. Condition (6) matters most because of that: everything here resolves through the existing identity package. If some part cannot, stop and file it as a finding rather than growing a second notion of who someone is — a chess-specific identity vocabulary alongside the host one would be worse than no browser login at all.\n\nWHY YOU. You built the chess identity lifecycle and the custody layer this sits on top of; the seat-anchoring semantics in particular are yours. The original was addressed to planner, I declined it on capacity, and that is part of how it was lost.\n\nIt carries no gate and is not urgent. I would rather it sat visibly unclaimed with the right owner than get lost a second time, which is the whole reason this row exists.","body":{"conditions":"Recovered work. Replaces 2c8c6d0489 in the core workroom, which was retired saying 'preserve its scope in history and refile it to the actual chess implementer after items 4 through 7 and the public identity boundaries land'. Those landed; no refile was ever made; hugh has confirmed identity integration is wanted and that chess is the right place for an initial version. SCOPE IS NARROWER THAN THE ORIGINAL because the substrate now exists — confirm this at your head before building. Chess already has identity.Resolve, identity.Identity with Witnessed and SelfSigned strengths, the anchor schema, seatAnchorQualifies, custody, and the anchor/revoke/list lifecycle merged at 0def23f. It has no OAuth, PKCE, callback, NIP-07, signEvent or browser credential code at all. What is missing is the front door, not the machinery. (1) Same-origin GitHub OAuth: state, PKCE, callback and token exchange, with fixed configurable provider endpoints so acceptance is deterministic. (2) A NIP-07 signEvent bridge over the exact host NostrDelegation bytes — do not invent a second serialisation. (3) An agent-key credential endorsement bounded by chess/game scope and expiry. (4) Two-axis anchored/unanchored identity display. (5) Fresh-session re-anchoring to the same identity after browser storage loss. (6) Do not invent a second identity vocabulary: everything above resolves through the existing identity package, and if it cannot, stop and file that as a finding rather than adding a parallel notion of who someone is. (7) SECRETS: tokens, witness root keys, private session keys and bearer credentials must never reach logs, errors, URLs or server storage. (8) Different, unanchored, expired or revoked identities must not recover a seat. (9) Mutation-sensitive service and UI tests for: callback state and origin, token redaction, both the proof and signature boundaries, old and new session fingerprints, same-identity recovered move accepted, wrong identity refused. Break each guard at its decision site, watch the named assertion fail, restore, confirm identical hashes. (10) Exact clean pushed head, per-path artifacts resting on your promise plus then-live bases, chess documentation updated, and docs/reference/architecture.md in the core workroom updated in the same head if a contract changes. (11) Independent review recording Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not merge. Not urgent — no gate, take it when your queue allows.","to":"09c97907402e7747d051c0c599d9eb463c48d19825820a3e4c433cde2e60e2a8"}}